MERSENNE PRIMES & EVEN PERFECT NUMBERS
512.72 Number theory

DEFINITIONS
  Mersenne number:     M_p = 2^p - 1     (p prime)
  Mersenne prime:      M_p itself prime
  Even perfect number: N = 2^{p-1} * (2^p - 1)
                       when M_p is prime

FUNDAMENTAL PROPERTY
  N equals the sum of its proper divisors.
  (A number is perfect if σ(N) - N = N, i.e. σ(N) = 2N)

EUCLID–EULER THEOREM
  Every even perfect number is of the form above.
  Euclid constructed them; Euler proved they exhaust all even cases.
  No odd perfect number is known.

EXAMPLES
  p=2:  2^1 * 3     = 6
  p=3:  2^2 * 7     = 28
  p=5:  2^4 * 31    = 496
  p=7:  2^6 * 127   = 8128

HISTORY
  Named after Marin Mersenne (1588–1644),
  who in 1644 listed candidate exponents p.
